  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 256 PM EDT Mon Aug 10 2026\n\nWednesday - Friday Night...\n\nUpper ridging will hold over south central CONUS through mid to late \nweek, and we'll continue to see a NW flow pattern over our region on \nthe NE periphery of the upper ridge. This will put us in the game \nfor continue MCS potential through late week, however much will \ndepend on the evolution of each previous system, and how far south \nand west into the ridge each convectively reinforced boundary / cold \npool can make it. For now based on pattern recognition, our NE half \nof the CWA looks to be the most likely to experience a severe \nweather threat (primarily damaging winds), along with potential \nflash flooding if repeated rounds of convection start to add up \nand/or, the western flank of any MCS stalls and becomes more \nparallel to the upper flow. \n\nAcross our SW CWA, we are expected to be deeper into the upper \nridge, and therefore expect an overall hotter and drier pattern. \nAlthough, a strong MCS could wreck this scenario at any point mid to \nlate week. For now, feel the more likely scenario will be better \nchances of getting into the low to mid 90s each day. These temps \ncombined with dew points expected to be in the low and mid 70s will \ncreate some heat index values peaking around 100 to 105 degrees. It \nis possible we could add a tier or two of counties to a Heat \nAdvisory along and west of I-65, but may have to take this a day at \na time as low confidence will continue with regard to MCS activity, \nand the potential to get a cold pool push or convective cloud debris \nwrecking a temp forecast.\n\nSaturday - Monday...\n\nThe upper ridge looks to build heading into the weekend, and center \nmore over the Ozarks. As this occurs, it will assert more influence \ninto our region, which should lead to a hotter/drier type of setup \nfor the weekend. The good news is that it should lessen the threat \nof MCS activity across our area, however it will likely come with \nhotter overall temps. Current NBM suggest low to mid 90s by Sunday. \nSome Heat Advisory headlines would likely be needed, at least for a \nportion of our area. Along and west of I-65 would be the best \ncandidate.\n\nBy Monday, the upper ridge may flatten out over our area and \nretrograde back to the west. As this occurs, a surface frontal \nboundary trailing from a NE CONUS system may drop into our northern \nCWA. We could be back in the game for more MCS activity around this \ntime, but not too confident this far out. Temps would likely settle \nback closer to normal highs for this time of year.",
